WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.963
Welcome back to Building
Global Secure Access.

2
00:00:02.963 --> 00:00:08.449
This is Chapter 7: Internet
Access, QUIC, and TLS inspection.

3
00:00:08.449 --> 00:00:11.687
Filtering the web by
identity is the easy part.

4
00:00:11.687 --> 00:00:16.067
The hard parts are the bypasses that
never expire, the protocol the service

5
00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:20.588
doesn't carry, and a certificate authority
that could impersonate every site.

6
00:00:20.588 --> 00:00:22.275
The code watches all three.

7
00:00:22.275 --> 00:00:26.300
This is also the chapter
where GCC availability bites.

8
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.814
The chapter is a draft awaiting review,

9
00:00:28.814 --> 00:00:32.700
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:37.525
Why these three?

11
00:00:37.525 --> 00:00:41.725
A bypass with no end date is a
permanent hole in the filter.

12
00:00:41.725 --> 00:00:43.851
QUIC isn't forwarded end to end,

13
00:00:43.851 --> 00:00:47.762
so a browser could carry web
traffic around the service.

14
00:00:47.762 --> 00:00:51.717
And a TLS inspection certificate
authority that's compromised

15
00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:54.925
could impersonate any site
to every managed device.

16
00:00:54.925 --> 00:00:59.572
So the goal: show that the ring's web
traffic is filtered by an identity-aware

17
00:00:59.572 --> 00:01:03.019
policy, that every exception
has an owner and an end date,

18
00:01:03.019 --> 00:01:07.966
that QUIC can't slip past it, and that
inspection runs from a dedicated authority,

19
00:01:07.966 --> 00:01:12.890
within an approved privacy scope,
breaking under one percent of sessions.

20
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.621
Learn's Internet Access tutorial creates
a filtering policy, a security profile,

21
00:01:21.621 --> 00:01:26.527
the link between them, and a Conditional
Access policy that assigns the profile.

22
00:01:26.527 --> 00:01:31.890
Its limits are generous, but Internet
Access doesn't forward QUIC end to end.

23
00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:35.991
Learn's newer pages block QUIC
with a cloud firewall rule,

24
00:01:35.991 --> 00:01:40.353
which is preview; its TLS page
uses Windows Firewall instead.

25
00:01:40.353 --> 00:01:44.802
Learn asks for an expiration or
review date on allow-list entries.

26
00:01:44.802 --> 00:01:49.804
The agency's inspection intermediate
needs specific extensions and at least

27
00:01:49.804 --> 00:01:54.652
six months' validity, only one is active,
and renewal starts ninety days ahead.

28
00:01:54.652 --> 00:01:58.852
Learn suggests a dedicated
authority, not the production PKI,

29
00:01:58.852 --> 00:02:03.400
and Microsoft treats failures
above one percent as systemic.

30
00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:09.313
Here's where GCC bites.

31
00:02:09.313 --> 00:02:13.103
In the guide's table, web
filtering, threat intelligence

32
00:02:13.103 --> 00:02:17.224
and TLS inspection rest on
Learn's product-wide GCC sentence.

33
00:02:17.224 --> 00:02:21.972
But the cloud firewall for clients,
the Explicit Forward Proxy and

34
00:02:21.972 --> 00:02:27.150
HTTP method filtering are previews
whose GCC availability isn't stated.

35
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.862
So the kit doesn't assume them.

36
00:02:28.862 --> 00:02:32.025
For QUIC, it expects the
guide's interim path:

37
00:02:32.025 --> 00:02:37.121
an Intune-delivered Windows Firewall
rule blocking outbound UDP 443,

38
00:02:37.121 --> 00:02:41.162
piloted first and recorded
in the architecture record.

39
00:02:41.162 --> 00:02:44.670
The QUIC check passes only
when that block is recorded,

40
00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:49.090
and a decision that leans on the
preview fails Chapter 1's check.

41
00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:53.890
Twelve checks.

42
00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:59.557
For filtering: each Internet Access policy
links an existing profile and excludes

43
00:02:59.557 --> 00:03:05.471
emergency access; the acceptable-use categories
and threat intelligence are blocked;

44
00:03:05.471 --> 00:03:08.592
every bypass is owned,
approved and unexpired;

45
00:03:08.592 --> 00:03:13.602
QUIC is blocked by the interim path;
and the tenant is inside Learn's limits.

46
00:03:13.602 --> 00:03:18.928
For TLS: more than ninety days left on a
certificate issued for at least six months;

47
00:03:18.928 --> 00:03:22.187
an issuer that isn't an
authentication authority;

48
00:03:22.187 --> 00:03:27.353
never the baseline profile before ring
3; the privacy approval and user notice

49
00:03:27.353 --> 00:03:32.202
recorded; every custom bypass registered;
and failures under one percent.

50
00:03:32.202 --> 00:03:35.966
One note: no Graph read for
TLS inspection was verified,

51
00:03:35.966 --> 00:03:38.740
so its record comes from the portal.

52
00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:43.540
Who's involved?

53
00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:47.358
The policy owner approves
categories and every bypass,

54
00:03:47.358 --> 00:03:49.765
and reviews bypasses each quarter.

55
00:03:49.765 --> 00:03:53.703
The privacy officer approves the
inspection scope before it starts.

56
00:03:53.703 --> 00:03:57.240
The PKI team runs the
dedicated issuing authority.

57
00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:02.165
The platform owner applies the changes and
renews the certificate ninety days ahead.

58
00:04:02.165 --> 00:04:06.840
And the security monitor watches the failure
rate and the top blocked destinations.

59
00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:11.469
For permissions: Network Access read
and write for the profiles and policies,

60
00:04:11.469 --> 00:04:15.663
Policy Read Write Conditional Access
for the Internet Access policy,

61
00:04:15.663 --> 00:04:19.340
and two administrator roles
activated just for the lab.

62
00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:25.426
Lab 1, after listing the reads,

63
00:04:25.426 --> 00:04:29.902
reading the GCC rows for Step
5, and collecting the pilot.

64
00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:32.614
Step 4 runs the Internet Access checks.

65
00:04:32.614 --> 00:04:36.202
The profile, the categories,
threat intelligence,

66
00:04:36.202 --> 00:04:39.527
the QUIC interim block
and the limits all pass.

67
00:04:39.527 --> 00:04:45.840
Two bypasses fail: E X 3 expired on
November first, and E X 4 has no approver.

68
00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.082
Step 5 reads the bypass rows
of the exception register,

69
00:04:50.082 --> 00:04:55.430
the agency's own record: target,
reason, owner, approver and expiry.

70
00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.618
Step 6 runs the TLS checks.

71
00:05:01.618 --> 00:05:02.629
Three fail.

72
00:05:02.629 --> 00:05:07.871
The certificate has seventy-four days
left, inside the ninety-day renewal window,

73
00:05:07.871 --> 00:05:12.017
and it was issued for only ninety
days, short of Learn's six months.

74
00:05:12.017 --> 00:05:16.467
The pinned payroll site's
bypass is E X 3, which expired.

75
00:05:16.467 --> 00:05:20.980
And one and a half percent of sessions
failed, above the one percent threshold.

76
00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:24.652
Three pass: the issuer is a
dedicated inspection authority,

77
00:05:24.652 --> 00:05:29.297
not the authentication one; the policy
is linked only to the pilot's profile;

78
00:05:29.297 --> 00:05:34.450
and the privacy approval and the user
notice came before inspection started.

79
00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:43.041
Step 7 checks both areas two weeks
later, after the certificate was rolled,

80
00:05:43.041 --> 00:05:47.962
the bypasses renewed and approved,
and the failing destinations bypassed.

81
00:05:47.962 --> 00:05:51.787
The certificate has a hundred and eighty
days left on two hundred and ten.

82
00:05:51.787 --> 00:05:54.887
Both bypasses have an
approver and a future date.

83
00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:56.963
The failure share is half a percent.

84
00:05:56.963 --> 00:05:58.700
All thirteen checks pass.

85
00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:03.150
Step 8 runs this chapter's
tests: six passed, none failed.

86
00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.350
Then delete the output folder.

87
00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:12.592
Lab 2 uses a test tenant with
Internet Access licenses,

88
00:06:12.592 --> 00:06:17.325
the Internet profile on for a pilot
group, and a test device with the client.

89
00:06:17.325 --> 00:06:19.417
It doesn't enable TLS inspection,

90
00:06:19.417 --> 00:06:23.987
which needs a dedicated test authority
and the privacy officer's scope.

91
00:06:23.987 --> 00:06:28.501
Create the filtering policy and the
pilot profile with the guide's requests.

92
00:06:28.501 --> 00:06:32.724
Plan and apply the Internet Access
policy, on for the pilot group only,

93
00:06:32.724 --> 00:06:37.688
as the guide does, because Learn doesn't
say whether report-only applies a profile.

94
00:06:37.688 --> 00:06:41.362
Block QUIC on the device with
Learn's firewall command.

95
00:06:41.362 --> 00:06:45.187
Then browse to a blocked category
and expect the block page.

96
00:06:45.187 --> 00:06:46.750
Back out newest first.

97
00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:49.120
The authors haven't run this lab.

98
00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:56.360
Identity-aware filtering
supports boundary protection,

99
00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:59.195
deny by default, and
malicious code protection.

100
00:06:59.195 --> 00:07:03.757
Governed bypasses support deny by
default and configuration settings.

101
00:07:03.757 --> 00:07:06.870
The QUIC block supports
boundary protection.

102
00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:10.223
And inspection from a
dedicated, renewed authority,

103
00:07:10.223 --> 00:07:15.136
within an approved privacy scope,
supports transmission confidentiality,

104
00:07:15.136 --> 00:07:18.333
key management, PKI
certificates and monitoring.

105
00:07:18.333 --> 00:07:23.244
The guide reads these steps as TIC
3.0's remote user pattern to the web.

106
00:07:23.244 --> 00:07:25.408
The assessor decides the controls.

107
00:07:25.408 --> 00:07:31.129
The dangers: assuming a preview firewall
exists in GCC, reusing the authentication

108
00:07:31.129 --> 00:07:36.270
authority, and letting the certificate
expire, which stops all inspection.

109
00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:42.033
You're done when every bypass is governed,
QUIC is blocked, and privacy came first.

110
00:07:42.033 --> 00:07:43.720
Next, Chapter 8.
